<!--
    A stub named <prefix>__<runtime name> reads as returning, so flow runs past the
    call. P2 lift and flow, option noreturn_known (default on). The script
    loads two linked ELFs whose stub callee, a bare ret, is named
    <prefix>__<multi-word list entry> and whose caller ends at that call.

    Asserts #1-#2 are the off-pass bug: flow runs past probe's symbol into padding,
    which fails to decode on AArch64 and decodes then trips funcboundflow on x86-64.
    Assert #3 is the on-pass fix on both architectures. Assert #4 keeps the plain
    name no-return. Asserts #5-#6 keep names that merely contain or end in a runtime
    name returning. Assert #7 renames the x86-64 stub mystack_chk_fail_count to keep
    a stub named for a single-word list entry returning. Assert #8 keeps a
    proxy-spelled name whose body is not a stub returning: keeps_session calls
    session__pthread_exit in the bytechunks; in each ELF keeps_count is pointed at
    keeps_exit, which is given that name, first as it is, then with an entry jump
    into its own body; on x86-64 handle_exit, the last mapped byte, is also given
    that name and turned into a ret imm16 that runs past the mapping. Assert #9
    keeps a proxy stub returning where its call has live code after it inside the
    caller: each ELF's mystack_chk_fail_count stub is renamed obj__stack_chk_fail.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x1010" readonly="true">e8fb0f0000b809000000c3</bytechunk>
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x2010" readonly="true">31c0c3</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x1010" name="keeps_session"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x2010" name="session__pthread_exit"/>
</binaryimage>
